# Required and optional form fields

- Explicitly mark every data-entry field in new and edited website forms as `(required)` or `(optional)` beside its persistent label. Apply the same convention to selection groups where users must provide a choice.
- Never leave users to infer a field's status from other labels, placeholders, color, or an unexplained asterisk.
- Labels must reflect actual validation. Use native `required` on supported required controls, or `aria-required` for appropriate custom controls, and enforce required values on the server when submitted.
- Do not make optional information mandatory just to standardize labels. Preserve the approved collection requirements.
- Keep the status in the accessible label and let long labels wrap on small screens.
- Cart example: Your name (required), Email address (required), Flagstaff pickup location (optional), Notes (optional).
- Research: https://baymard.com/research-articles/required-optional-form-fields
